# SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0
#
# Worked example — four gmeow:logic advantages over gUFO / OWL 2, each
# demonstrating one of the named OWL-2 criticisms (LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md §OWL 2
# criticisms). The logic: terms used here are the foundation surface authored in
# #663; the native engine that EXECUTES them (rules, chase, builtins) is seamed
# to #664/#665, so this file demonstrates the SHAPE of each fix, not its
# evaluation.
#
#   §1  Native edge property (triple-bloat fix, criticism 3)
#       An employment edge carries its validFrom/validTo time scope ON THE EDGE
#       via an RDF-1.2 reifier — NOT by minting a gUFO Relator/Situation node.
#       gUFO needs a reified TemporaryRelationshipSituation (a whole blank-node
#       subgraph) to time-scope a relationship; gmeow:logic types the reifier a
#       logic:Fluent (a time-indexed relation) and hangs the interval on it.
#
#   §2  Strict partial order (OWL-2 global-restriction fix, criticism 7)
#       logic:properPartOf is typed transitive ∧ asymmetric ∧ irreflexive at
#       once. OWL 2 DL FORBIDS this combination (transitive properties may not
#       be irreflexive/asymmetric, the global cardinality/anti-symmetry
#       restriction) — gmeow:logic carries it natively, so a genuine strict
#       partial order over parthood is expressible.
#
#   §3  Multi-level instantiation (no punning, criticism 5)
#       :marv is an instance of :goldenEagle, which is ITSELF an instance of
#       :species. OWL 2 forces punning (the same IRI used as both class and
#       individual) to fake this; logic:instanceOf reifies types as first-order
#       individuals so the chain is ordinary first-order quantification, with
#       logic:orderedType recording each level (HiLog deep instantiation).
#
#   §4  Builtin-derived value (criticism 8)
#       A derivation references the logic:builtinArithmetic primitive to compute
#       legalAge — the lightweight in-logic computation OWL 2 has no surface for
#       (it can classify but not CALCULATE). The builtin individual is declared
#       in the logic: module; this links a derivation to it via
#       logic:invokesBuiltin.

@prefix gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/> .
@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# ===========================================================================
# §1  Native edge property — triple-bloat fix (criticism 3)
# ===========================================================================
# The relationship itself is a plain edge:
ex:alice ex:employedBy ex:acme .

# The time scope rides ON THE EDGE through an RDF-1.2 reifier. The canonical
# RDF-1.2 triple-term form (the lead artifact, emitted by Apache Jena — see
# generated/statements/gmeow.rdf12.ttl) is:
#
#     ex:employment-alice-acme rdf:reifies <<( ex:alice ex:employedBy ex:acme )>> .
#
# rdflib (the validation/docs toolchain, RDF-1.2-triple-terms unsupported) reads
# the structurally-isomorphic RDF reification of that triple term — the same
# reifier node, the same quoted (subject, predicate, object), just spelled with
# the rdf:subject/rdf:predicate/rdf:object vocabulary the triple term desugars
# to. The reifier is typed logic:Fluent: a time-indexed relation, the native
# four-dimensional construct that REPLACES gUFO's reified
# TemporaryRelationshipSituation blank-node subgraph.
ex:employment-alice-acme
    a rdf:Statement, logic:Fluent ;
    rdfs:label "Alice's employment at Acme, scoped to 2020-03-01 .. 2024-08-15"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdf:subject   ex:alice ;
    rdf:predicate ex:employedBy ;
    rdf:object    ex:acme ;
    ex:validFrom  "2020-03-01"^^xsd:date ;
    ex:validTo    "2024-08-15"^^xsd:date .

# ===========================================================================
# §2  Strict partial order — OWL-2 global-restriction fix (criticism 7)
# ===========================================================================
# logic:properPartOf is declared in the module typed transitive ∧ asymmetric ∧
# irreflexive — the genuine strict partial order OWL 2 DL forbids. A parthood
# chain over a bicycle: the spoke is a proper part of the wheel, the wheel of
# the bicycle. Under transitivity the spoke is therefore a proper part of the
# bicycle; under irreflexivity nothing is a proper part of itself; under
# asymmetry the relation never runs both ways. (Materialization of the
# transitive consequence is the engine's job, #664/#665.)
ex:spoke   logic:properPartOf ex:wheel .
ex:wheel   logic:properPartOf ex:bicycle .

# ===========================================================================
# §3  Multi-level instantiation — no punning (criticism 5)
# ===========================================================================
# A two-step logic:instanceOf chain across three orders, with logic:orderedType
# making each level explicit. :marv (an order-0 individual) instantiates
# :goldenEagle (an order-1 type), which in turn instantiates :species (an
# order-2 metatype). No IRI is used as both class and individual: every type is
# a first-order logic:Type individual, so the chain is plain quantification.
ex:species
    a logic:Type ;
    rdfs:label "the metatype whose instances are biological species"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:orderedType 2 .

ex:goldenEagle
    a logic:Type ;
    rdfs:label "the golden eagle species"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:instanceOf  ex:species ;
    logic:orderedType 1 .

ex:marv
    rdfs:label "Marv, an individual golden eagle"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:instanceOf  ex:goldenEagle ;
    logic:orderedType 0 .

# ===========================================================================
# §4  Builtin-derived value (criticism 8)
# ===========================================================================
# A derivation that computes a person's legal age from a birth date references
# the logic:builtinArithmetic primitive (declared in the logic: module) via
# logic:invokesBuiltin. OWL 2 can classify but cannot CALCULATE; this is the
# lightweight in-logic computation (Principle 12) the foundation needs. No
# logic:Rule class is minted (the surface is foundation-only), so — exactly as
# in projection-loss-ledger.ttl — the derivation is modelled as a
# gmeow:InformationObject and linked to the REAL builtin individual.
ex:derivation-legalAge
    a gmeow:InformationObject ;
    rdfs:label "derive legalAge = floor((today - birthDate) / 1 year) >= majority"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:invokesBuiltin logic:builtinArithmetic .
